\section{Shock Modeling}

Shock index:
\[
S_{\text{shock}}(t)
=
\omega_1 L(t)
+
\omega_2 \iota(t)
+
\omega_3 \frac{1}{H(t) + \epsilon}.
\]

Shock progression:
\[
\frac{dS_{\text{shock}}}{dt}
=
\alpha_S S_{\text{inj}}
+
\beta_S P(t)
+
\gamma_S s(t)
-
\delta_S S_{\text{shock}},
\]
where $s(t)$ is senescence burden (Module D).

\section{Clinical Assimilation Layer}

Clinical measurements:
\[
\mathbf{z}_k
=
\big(
\text{Lactate}_k,\,
\text{ABG}_k,\,
\text{CBC}_k
\big).
\]

Observation model:
\[
\mathbf{z}_k
=
H_C \mathbf{S}_C(t_k)
+
\epsilon_k.
\]

Assimilation via UKF:
\[
\mathbf{S}_C(t_k^+)
=
\mathbf{S}_C(t_k^-)
+
K_k
\big(
\mathbf{z}_k - H_C\mathbf{S}_C(t_k^-)
\big).
\]

\section{Coupling to Module A}

Inflammation influenced by genetic dysregulation:
\[
\iota(t)
\leftarrow
\iota(t)
+
\lambda_A \sum_i g_i(t).
\]

\section{Coupling to Module B}

Pathogen load increases trauma severity:
\[
S_{\text{inj}}(t)
=
S_{\text{inj}}^{(0)}
+
\lambda_B P(t).
\]

\section{Coupling to Module D}

Senescence reduces recovery:
\[
\beta_H(t)
=
\beta_H^{(0)}
-
\lambda_D s(t).
\]

\section{Numerical Integration}

Hybrid solver:
\[
\mathbf{z}(t_{k+1})
=
\mathbf{z}(t_k)
+
\Delta t_k\, \Phi_C(\mathbf{z}(t_k), t_k).
\]

Projection:
\[
H(t), O(t), M(t) \leftarrow \max(\cdot, 0).
\]

Adaptive step:
\[
\Delta t_{k+1}
=
\Delta t_k \cdot \psi(\epsilon_k).
\]

\section{Stress Testing}

Hemodynamic collapse:
\[
H_{\text{stress}}(t)
=
0.3 H(t).
\]

Hyperinflammation:
\[
\iota_{\text{stress}}
=
5 \iota(t).
\]

Severe hypoxia:
\[
O_{\text{stress}}
=
0.2 O(t).
\]

\section{Module Output}

Module C outputs:
\[
\mathbf{S}_C(t)
=
\big(
H(t),\,
O(t),\,
L(t),\,
\iota(t),\,
M(t)
\big),
\]
which feeds:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Module D (senescence),
    \item countermeasure optimization,
    \item global shock index.
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}

Module C provides a multiscale model of trauma physiology, integrating
hemodynamic instability, oxygen transport failure, lactate accumulation,
inflammatory cascades, and metabolic disruption. Its coupling with Modules A, B,
and D makes it a central component of the MPS--PCE system for predicting
trauma‑driven deterioration and optimizing countermeasures.